window.SLTT_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"url":"about.html","title":"About","h1":"…land that we love.","description":"Mission, vision, purpose and principles of Sierra Leone To The Top.","category":"Page","text":"About the platform …land that we love. Sierra Leone To The Top exists to help move Sierra Leone forward. Foundation v1.0 Our mission To promote, connect and support people, ideas, initiatives and opportunities that contribute to the advancement of Sierra Leone. Our vision A confident, connected, innovative and prosperous Sierra Leone in which its people have the opportunity and capacity to reach their full potential. Our purpose Sierra Leone To The Top is a platform for promoting the people, ideas, achievements, opportunities, institutions, businesses, communities and initiatives that can contribute to a stronger and more prosperous Sierra Leone. Progress should be promoted, connected and multiplied. Our principles Sierra Leone first We ask whether the work contributes positively to Sierra Leone or Sierra Leoneans. Independent and nonpartisan We are not an arm of a political party, government, opposition movement or politician. Good ideas can come from anywhere and problems should be examined on evidence. Constructive Challenges should be discussed honestly, but whenever possible criticism should point toward solutions. Inclusive The platform belongs to a vision of Sierra Leone that includes every region, community, generation and Sierra Leonean around the world. Credible Claims should be accurate, achievements should not be exaggerated and relationships should never be implied where none exist. Action-oriented The platform should ultimately be judged by what it helps make possible, not simply by how much content it publishes. What we are not We are not a political party, government agency, propaganda platform or a brand centered on one individual. We are building a platform for Sierra Leonean progress. A platform with history Relaunched, not invented yesterday. The current platform is broader and more structured, but the Sierra Leone To The Top name and media activity have a documented history. 2017 Video blog + early commercial use Preserved Facebook links document the Sierra Leone To The Top Video Blog publishing in July 2017. A later U.S. trademark application separately recorded September 28, 2017 as the claimed first-use date for apparel. Explore the 2017 archive → 2020 Video and conversation The Sierra Leone To The Top YouTube archive preserves a run of IG Live conversation titles from May and June 2020. A U.S. trademark application for the name was filed that same year. 2026 A broader relaunch The brand is being rebuilt as an independent national-progress platform spanning people, business, culture, places, communities, diaspora, youth and ideas. Historical note: the 2017 and 2020 brand-history dates above are based on the public trademark filing record and the existing Sierra Leone To The Top YouTube archive. This timeline describes brand history; it does not imply that the prior trademark application is currently active. How we work Trust is part of the product. We publish with explicit rules for verification, corrections, archive material, sponsorships, media rights and AI-assisted tools. Read our editorial standards Independent National pride without partisan loyalty. The platform can praise progress, identify problems and examine ideas without becoming an arm of a political party, government or opposition movement."},{"url":"accessibility.html","title":"Accessibility","h1":"Accessibility","description":"Accessibility commitment and reporting information for Sierra Leone To The Top.","category":"Page","text":"Site commitment Accessibility Sierra Leone To The Top should be usable by as many people as practical, across devices, connection speeds and ways of navigating the web. Our target We aim toward WCAG 2.2 Level AA practices as the site develops. This is a target and design standard — not a claim that every page has completed a formal third-party accessibility audit. What is built into the current site Semantic page structure and a consistent main-content landmark. A “Skip to content” link for keyboard users. Keyboard-accessible navigation and native links/buttons. Responsive layouts designed to reflow on phones and smaller screens. Text alternatives for meaningful photographs and visual content. Color choices designed for readable contrast rather than decoration alone. No essential interaction that depends only on hover. Low-bandwidth usability matters too Accessibility in Sierra Leone also includes practical network conditions. The site is being built as a lightweight static website without a heavy application framework, and we will continue to optimize images and avoid unnecessary scripts. Video and social content As new original video is produced, the goal is to add useful titles, descriptions and captions or transcripts where practical. Archive material may not always meet the same production standard, and should be clearly identified as archive content. Report a problem If something prevents you from reading, navigating or understanding the site, we want to hear about it. Please identify the page, the problem and — if helpful — the device or assistive technology involved. Contact us about accessibility Continuous improvement Accessibility is not a one-time checklist. New content, forms, maps, video players and third-party integrations will need to be reviewed as they are introduced."},{"url":"archive.html","title":"Archive","h1":"The Sierra Leone To The Top Archive.","description":"The Sierra Leone To The Top archive: documented media and brand history from 2017 onward.","category":"Archive","text":"Preserve the record The Sierra Leone To The Top Archive. Old work is not new reporting. But it is part of the story — and it deserves to be preserved, labeled and understood in context. 2017 Facebook · Video Blog The early public video-blog era. Preserved July 2017 posts document Sierra Leone To The Top publishing everyday Sierra Leone video material under the brand. Explore 2017 → 2020 Instagram Live · YouTube The Conversations. A concentrated run of live-video titles placed Sierra Leonean creatives and personalities in conversation under the Sierra Leone To The Top name. Explore 2020 → 2026 Field archive · Relaunch From archive to editorial system. The relaunch preserves earlier material while adding sourcing, corrections, rights tracking and a broader national-progress framework. See the Lumley archive story →"},{"url":"business.html","title":"Business & Innovation","h1":"Business & Innovation","description":"Explore Sierra Leonean entrepreneurship, businesses, technology and economic opportunity.","category":"Page","text":"Core pillar Business & Innovation What are Sierra Leoneans building? Built in Sierra Leone This section will make enterprise visible: the people creating products, employing people, solving local problems, applying technology and finding ways to grow in Sierra Leone’s real operating environment. Promotion is earned by a real story, not purchased by a claim. What a strong business feature needs The problem What need does the business address? The product What does it actually make or provide? The evidence What can we verify about its operation? The lesson What can another builder learn? Coverage map Follow where value is being created. Not a ranking. Not an advertisement directory. A reporting map of sectors, makers and useful ideas. Make Products & manufacturing What is being produced locally and what it takes to scale. Grow Agriculture & food Farms, processing, distribution and value added in-country. Build Technology Digital tools and services solving practical problems. Create Creative economy Fashion, design, media, music and other businesses built on ideas. Serve Small business The everyday enterprises that make communities work. Connect Diaspora enterprise Businesses bridging Sierra Leone with customers, capital and networks abroad. Nominate a business story Business Radar · 2026 Five startups. Five different problems to solve. READY Salone’s recent top-five announcement spans small-business finance, bioenergy, urban waste, agriculture and localized AI. Read the briefing Our next step Move from pitch descriptions to operating stories. Future profiles should talk to founders, users and other relevant sources so we can understand what is actually being built, where it works and what is difficult."},{"url":"communities.html","title":"Communities & Development","h1":"Communities & Development","description":"See practical work and local solutions helping Sierra Leonean communities move forward.","category":"Page","text":"Core pillar Communities & Development What is working on the ground? What you’ll find here This section focuses on action: education, sanitation, environment, infrastructure, agriculture, civic initiatives, technology for development and other practical work improving communities. The emphasis is progress, evidence and useful lessons. What is working on the ground? What belongs here Community stories Local solutions Environment Education Development How it will work Stories should be accurate, visually strong and useful. Each substantial feature can be adapted into a website story, a YouTube or short-form video, social posts and shareable excerpts so one piece of reporting can travel across the Sierra Leone To The Top ecosystem. From the Archive: a visible result at Lumley Beach → This section will grow as we document practical community action and the people behind it."},{"url":"contact.html","title":"Contact","h1":"Start with the right channel.","description":"Contact Sierra Leone To The Top for story leads, corrections, media inquiries and platform questions.","category":"Page","text":"Contact Start with the right channel. Story leads, corrections, media questions and general inquiries should be easy to route without creating unnecessary meetings. Story leads Know something worth covering? Nominate a person, business, place, community initiative, opportunity or idea. A lead is an invitation to investigate — not a guarantee of coverage. Share a story lead Corrections See a factual problem? We want to know. Our corrections standard is to fix confirmed material errors rather than protect them. Read the corrections policy Verified social channel X · @SL2TheTop For public contact while the new website domain and official email are being finalized. Open X Video Sierra Leone To The Top TV Visit the existing YouTube channel and the archive while new original programming is developed. Open YouTube Longstanding public page Facebook · Sierra Leone To The Top The public Facebook identity is part of the documented brand history going back to 2017. Open Facebook Longstanding public identity Instagram · @sierraleonetothetop The handle appears in historical Sierra Leone To The Top video and correspondence records. Open Instagram Official email Domain email comes next. The official Sierra Leone To The Top domain is not connected yet. When it is activated, this page will be updated with the permanent institutional email rather than publishing a temporary address that would later need to be replaced."},{"url":"contribute.html","title":"Contribute","h1":"Help us find what is moving Sierra Leone forward.","description":"Nominate people, businesses, places, opportunities and ideas for Sierra Leone To The Top.","category":"Page","text":"Contribute Help us find what is moving Sierra Leone forward. Sierra Leone To The Top is being built to discover and amplify worthwhile people, businesses, places, opportunities, community work and ideas. Story leads & nominations What you can nominate People Businesses Places Community work Diaspora Youth opportunities Ideas & solutions What makes a strong lead A clear connection to Sierra Leone or Sierra Leoneans. Something concrete that can be verified. A useful, interesting or constructive story for the public. Enough information for us to identify and contact the person or organization. Submission channel Story nominations will open with the public launch. In the meantime, follow Sierra Leone To The Top on our connected channels and begin thinking about the people, businesses, places and ideas that deserve wider attention. Follow on X Visit YouTube Editorial independence A nomination is a lead, not a promise of coverage. Sierra Leone To The Top will verify material before publication and retains editorial independence over what it features. Something else? Use the Contact page. Corrections, platform questions and other inquiries should go through the appropriate contact route rather than being submitted as story nominations. Contact Sierra Leone To The Top"},{"url":"credits.html","title":"Credits","h1":"Use great media. Credit it properly.","description":"Brand, media and image credits for Sierra Leone To The Top.","category":"Page","text":"Credits & media policy Use great media. Credit it properly. This page records brand and third-party media attribution used by Sierra Leone To The Top. Current build Official brand asset The Sierra Leone To The Top logo used throughout this site is the approved official brand logo supplied by the project. Photography policy We prefer original Sierra Leone To The Top photography, contributor photography used with permission, public-domain material, or clearly licensed Creative Commons media with the required attribution. We do not treat an image being publicly visible online as permission to reuse it. Original photography The Lumley Beach shoreline image used on the Home and Discover pages comes from a Sierra Leone To The Top-controlled field archive. Web-ready derivatives are stored with the site. Historical archive references The 2020 Conversations index is based on titles and dates preserved by the existing Sierra Leone To The Top YouTube channel. Guest biographies and additional claims are intentionally excluded until separately verified. Kelvin Doe profile sources The Kelvin Doe profile uses Sierra Leone's Ministry of Communication, Technology and Innovation for the January 2026 robotics tournament, a July 2026 long-form profile for current context, and contemporaneous 2012 reporting for his early maker story. Claims are attributed rather than presented as independent audit findings. Diaspora & heritage sources The Bunce Island/Gullah Geechee feature uses Sierra Leone Heritage for the site’s protection and homecoming history, the U.S. National Park Service for Gullah Geechee cultural context, UNESCO’s Bunce Island tentative-list entry for the Sierra Leone/U.S. heritage connection, and Smithsonian material for the history of Krio/Gullah linguistic scholarship. Culture explainer sources The Krio explainer draws on Sierra Leone’s Ministry of Tourism and Cultural Affairs, Sierra Leone Heritage, the Permanent Mission of Sierra Leone, Smithsonian collections and academic work on Krio and national identity. It is presented as an introductory synthesis rather than a definitive linguistic history. Next Generation case-study source The Learn2Earn analysis attributes participant and program facts to the Ministry of Communication, Technology and Innovation’s July 14, 2026 profile of James Emmanuel Paul Yambasu. The four lessons presented on Sierra Leone To The Top are our analysis of the reported sequence rather than quotations or independent verification of the employment relationship. Business Radar sources The READY Salone startup briefing summarizes the Sierra Leone Ministry of Communication, Technology and Innovation’s August 3, 2026 public announcement. Additional company self-descriptions may be consulted during follow-up reporting, but the launch article intentionally distinguishes ministry-reported information from independent verification. Editorial policy See Editorial Standards for verification, corrections, archive, sponsorship, AI-assisted production and media-rights rules. Third-party media Where third-party media is used, photographer, source and license or permission details will be recorded here or alongside the relevant story. Future attribution When licensed external photographs are introduced, photographer, source and license details will be recorded here and, where useful, next to the relevant story."},{"url":"culture.html","title":"Culture & Identity","h1":"Culture & Identity","description":"Celebrate Sierra Leonean culture, history, creativity and identity.","category":"Page","text":"Core pillar Culture & Identity What makes Sierra Leone Sierra Leone? Living culture Culture is not a museum piece. It is living, changing and global — expressed through music, food, languages, fashion, literature, art, history, dance, humor, memory and everyday life. Celebrate identity without freezing it in the past. What we want to capture Food Music History Languages Fashion Art Traditions Diaspora culture Archive · 2020 Creative voices were already part of Sierra Leone To The Top. The preserved IG Live titles include a concentrated run of conversations from May and June 2020. The relaunch keeps that history visible while creating a higher editorial standard for new work. Explore the archive New series Living Culture Short documentaries and features that explain not only what Sierra Leoneans create, but where traditions, sounds, foods, words and styles come from — and how they are changing. Story formats Culture people can enter. Useful culture coverage gives visitors something to hear, taste, see, understand or remember. Listen Music & sound Artists, producers, instruments, scenes and the stories behind songs. Taste Food & memory Dishes, ingredients, markets, cooks and how food travels across families and borders. Speak Language Krio and Sierra Leone’s other languages as living tools of identity and expression. Remember History Places, objects, oral history and context that help explain the country we see today. Krio language · identity · connection Living Culture A local language became a national bridge. Our first evergreen culture explainer looks at Krio’s Freetown roots, its role across Sierra Leone and why its story reaches beyond a tourist phrasebook. Read the Krio explainer"},{"url":"diaspora.html","title":"Diaspora","h1":"Diaspora","description":"Connect Sierra Leone’s global community with knowledge, networks, opportunity and home.","category":"Page","text":"Core pillar Diaspora Sierra Leone is larger than its borders. Global Sierra Leone The diaspora is not simply a source of remittances. It is a network of knowledge, professional experience, relationships, capital, creativity and people who remain part of Sierra Leone’s story wherever they live. Connection should make participation easier, not more ceremonial. Five practical pathways Return Stories of moving back, visiting with purpose, or building across two homes. Mentor Transfer skills and experience to people who can use them. Build Create businesses, projects, networks and professional bridges. Invest Understand opportunities with evidence rather than hype. Connect Open doors to markets, institutions, expertise and relationships. Diaspora stories What did you learn away — and what can travel back? Profiles should focus on practical experience: careers, businesses, return decisions, professional networks and lessons that can be useful to others. Two-way connection Sierra Leone has something to offer the diaspora too. Culture, belonging, opportunity, collaboration and a chance to participate in building something meaningful. Share a diaspora lead Diaspora · Heritage Some connections cross centuries. Bunce Island and documented Gullah homecoming visits show that diaspora connection can be about memory, history and cultural return — not only economics. Read the heritage feature A reciprocal relationship Ask what Sierra Leone and its global descendants can understand together. The diaspora pillar should make room for investment and professional networks, but also history, identity, culture and belonging."},{"url":"discover.html","title":"Discover Sierra Leone","h1":"Discover Sierra Leone","description":"Explore Sierra Leone’s places, nature, communities, heritage and local experiences.","category":"Page","text":"Core pillar Discover Sierra Leone Why should people discover Sierra Leone? Lumley Beach, Freetown · Photo: Sierra Leone To The Top archive. What you’ll find here This will become a practical, story-rich guide to experiencing Sierra Leone: beaches, islands, mountains, forests, wildlife, neighborhoods, heritage sites, restaurants, hotels and local experiences — told with local context. Why should people discover Sierra Leone? What belongs here Destinations Travel guides Food Nature Heritage Itineraries How it will work Stories should be accurate, visually strong and useful. Each substantial feature can be adapted into a website story, a YouTube or short-form video, social posts and shareable excerpts so one piece of reporting can travel across the Sierra Leone To The Top ecosystem. Read: From the Archive — Lumley Beach → This section will grow into practical destination guides, local recommendations and stories from across Sierra Leone. Start here Six places. Six different sides of Sierra Leone. A sourced first-look guide spanning city life, conservation, heritage, rainforest, islands and the wild interior. 01 Freetown Capital + coast 02 Tacugama Conservation 03 Bunce Island Heritage 04 Tiwai Island Rainforest 05 Banana Islands Island life 06 Outamba-Kilimi Wild interior Read the first-look guide"},{"url":"guide-six-ways-to-discover.html","title":"Six ways to discover Sierra Leone","h1":"Six places. Six different sides of Sierra Leone.","description":"A sourced first-look guide to six different sides of Sierra Leone: Freetown, Tacugama, Bunce Island, Tiwai Island, Banana Islands and Outamba-Kilimi.","category":"Page","text":"Discover Sierra Leone · First look Six places. Six different sides of Sierra Leone. A city and coast. A conservation sanctuary. A site of global history. A rainforest island. An Atlantic archipelago. A national park. Sierra Leone cannot be understood through one kind of destination. Planning guide · Based on official and destination sources Lumley Beach, Freetown · Photo: Sierra Leone To The Top archive. Before you travel Access, operating hours, permits, transport and services can change. Use this as a first-look guide, then verify current arrangements with the destination or Sierra Leone’s official tourism bodies. National Tourist Board portal ↗ Sierra Leone’s strongest travel story is variety. You can move from Freetown and the peninsula coast into rainforest, wildlife, islands and sites that connect Sierra Leone to the wider history of the Atlantic world. 01 Capital + coast Freetown The National Tourist Board describes Freetown through its markets, beaches and historical landmarks. For many visitors it is the natural starting point — city life on one side, the Western Area Peninsula and Atlantic coast on the other. Official tourism source ↗ 02 Conservation Tacugama Chimpanzee Sanctuary Tacugama sits within the Western Area forest landscape outside Freetown. Sierra Leone’s Ministry of Tourism says the sanctuary opened in 1995; today Tacugama combines chimpanzee conservation, education, forest protection and visitor experiences. Ministry source ↗ Tacugama ↗ 03 Heritage Bunce Island About 32 kilometers upriver from Freetown, Bunce Island preserves the remains of a British slave-trading fortress. It is a place for historical memory and reflection, especially within the wider story connecting Sierra Leone and descendants of enslaved Africans in the Americas. Ministry source ↗ Sierra Leone Heritage ↗ Read the deeper diaspora/history feature → 04 Rainforest island Tiwai Island Wildlife Sanctuary Tiwai lies between two arms of the Moa River and covers about 1,200 hectares. Official tourism information says it was gazetted as a wildlife sanctuary in 1987. Tiwai’s own visitor program frames ecotourism as a way to experience the rainforest while supporting conservation and surrounding communities. Ministry source ↗ Tiwai visitor information ↗ 05 Island life Banana Islands Southwest of the Freetown Peninsula, the Banana Islands comprise Dublin, Ricketts and uninhabited Mes-Meheux. The Ministry of Tourism describes Dublin for its beaches and Ricketts for its forests, with fishing and tourism central to island life. Ministry source ↗ 06 Wild interior Outamba-Kilimi National Park Declared a national park in 1995, Outamba-Kilimi consists of two areas separated by a corridor and combines lowland forest, moist savannah woodland, grassland and gallery forest. The Ministry lists primates, elephants, hippopotamuses and other wildlife among the park’s fauna. Ministry source ↗ One country, many entry points This list is intentionally not a “top six.” Sierra Leone has more beaches, islands, forests, mountains, heritage sites, communities and experiences than any short list can represent. The purpose is to show the range — and create a structure we can expand with original reporting. Editorial note: descriptions above are independently summarized from the linked official or destination sources. Sierra Leone To The Top has not treated promotional claims as independently verified simply because they appear on a tourism website. Return to Discover Sierra Leone →"},{"url":"ideas.html","title":"Ideas & Solutions","h1":"Ideas & Solutions","description":"Constructive analysis and practical ideas for Sierra Leone’s future.","category":"Page","text":"Core pillar Ideas & Solutions How can Sierra Leone do things better? Constructive by design This is where difficult subjects can be examined without turning the platform into either a complaint machine or a collection of unrealistic promises. Constructive does not mean avoiding problems. It means doing the work required to move beyond them. The operating question Every substantial Ideas & Solutions feature should start by making the problem specific, establishing what evidence exists, understanding what is already being tried, confronting real-world constraints and identifying a useful next action. Problem Evidence Existing work Constraints Action + measurement Read the five-part method Coverage Subjects where better questions matter. We do not need to have the answer before beginning. We do need a question that can be investigated fairly. Move Transport & cities Mobility, roads, public space, planning and how people move through communities. Power Energy Reliability, access, cost, generation, maintenance and productive use. Learn Education & skills From classroom outcomes to technical training and employability. Grow Agriculture & food Production, storage, processing, markets, soil and value chains. Build Technology & services Digital systems that solve actual problems rather than technology for display. Serve Institutions & public systems How rules, processes, capacity and accountability affect implementation. How We Move Forward Problem → Evidence → Existing work → Constraints → Action. A repeatable editorial method for turning public discussion into something that can be tested, challenged and improved. See how it works No automatic consensus Good-faith disagreement belongs here. When credible people disagree about diagnosis, evidence, tradeoffs or policy, the platform should show readers where the disagreement actually lies rather than manufacturing false certainty."},{"url":"index.html","title":"Home","h1":"People. Ideas. Opportunity. Progress.","description":"A platform showcasing and connecting the people, businesses, places, culture and solutions moving Sierra Leone forward.","category":"Page","text":"Sierra Leone, moving forward People. Ideas. Opportunity. Progress. Sierra Leone To The Top is a platform showcasing and connecting the people, businesses, places, culture and solutions moving Sierra Leone forward. Discover Sierra Leone Watch the stories Our purpose Celebrate what is working. Connect what can grow. Help move Sierra Leone forward. National pride without propaganda. Constructive ideas without partisanship. Progress made visible. Eight ways forward One platform. A whole country of possibility. We organize the platform around the people, places, ideas and opportunities that shape Sierra Leone’s future. 01 · People People & Stories Meet the Sierra Leoneans building, creating, leading and making a difference. 02 · Build Business & Innovation Entrepreneurs, products, technology and opportunities made in Sierra Leone. 03 · Identity Culture & Identity Food, music, history, language, creativity and what makes Sierra Leone Sierra Leone. 04 · Explore Discover Sierra Leone Beaches, communities, heritage, nature and experiences worth knowing. 05 · Action Communities & Development Practical work, local progress and solutions improving lives. 06 · Connect Diaspora Connecting Sierra Leone’s global community with knowledge, networks and opportunity. 07 · Future Youth & Next Generation Education, skills, technology, careers and opportunities for what comes next. 08 · Solve Ideas & Solutions Constructive discussion about how Sierra Leone can do things better. From the archive Progress is worth documenting. Sierra Leone To The Top will build a growing record of people, places and practical action that show the country in motion. Freetown · April 2026 From the Archive: Lumley Beach A clean shoreline image from Lumley Beach captures the kind of visible, practical progress this platform exists to notice, document and share. Read the archive story → Explore all stories Discover Sierra Leone A country worth seeing — and seeing differently. From Freetown’s coastline to communities, heritage sites, food, nature and local experiences, Discover Sierra Leone will help Sierra Leoneans and visitors explore the country with curiosity and respect. Start discovering Places Travel and local experiences People Guides and local voices Stories History and living culture Sierra Leone To The Top TV Watch the story unfold. YouTube will be a major storytelling engine: interviews, field stories, travel, culture, business profiles and explainers. Sierra Leone To The Top TV Original stories. Sierra Leonean voices. A wider view of the country. Visit Watch Format 01 Meet a Sierra Leonean Profiles and conversations with people doing meaningful work. Format 02 Discover Travel, culture, food, places and local experiences. Format 03 Ideas Clear, constructive explainers about problems and solutions. Latest from the platform Stories, guides and ideas worth opening. One compact editorial shelf can keep growing without turning the homepage into a new full-width section for every story. Business Radar Five startups. Five different problems to solve. Small-business tools, bioenergy, waste, agriculture and localized AI. Next Generation Training was only the first step. Four lessons about skills, evidence, professional visibility and mentorship. Living Culture Krio: a language of connection. Freetown history, national connection and a wider Atlantic story. Diaspora · Heritage Bunce Island and the meaning of return. Gullah Geechee connection, memory and documented homecoming visits. Discover Six ways to discover Sierra Leone. City, conservation, heritage, rainforest, islands and the wild interior. Ideas & Solutions How We Move Forward. A five-part test for moving from complaint to measurable action. Explore all stories Help us find the next story → Documented history The story reaches back to 2017. Preserved Facebook video-blog posts, a 2020 live-video archive and the current relaunch now sit inside one clearly labeled historical record. Explore the archive How we earn trust Celebrate Sierra Leone. Keep the standard high. Our public rules cover sourcing, corrections, archive material, sponsorships, media rights, AI-assisted tools and nonpartisanship. Verify Claims need evidence. Label Archive and sponsored material stay clear. Correct Confirmed errors get fixed. Separate National pride is not party loyalty. Read our editorial standards …land that we love Sierra Leone To The Top. Promoting people, ideas, opportunities and action that move Sierra Leone forward. Read our purpose"},{"url":"people.html","title":"People & Stories","h1":"People & Stories","description":"Meet Sierra Leoneans building, creating, leading and making a difference.","category":"Page","text":"Core pillar People & Stories Who is moving Sierra Leone forward? People first Sierra Leoneans should be at the center of Sierra Leone’s story. We profile people for what they are building, creating, teaching, leading, learning or solving — not simply because they are well known. Who is moving Sierra Leone forward? Our profile test Specific What has this person actually done? Verified What can we substantiate? Human What can others learn from the journey? Useful Why does the story matter now? Archive collection The 2020 Conversations. The existing Sierra Leone To The Top channel preserves an early run of live-video titles. We are cataloguing that history before adding new context. AJ Money Sour Fight Lil Skanky TheRealBamSavage M.I.C. Rich Blink Adfega Jr. EDJ KingSLZee ZenoSalone Explore the 2020 archive New reporting Meet a Sierra Leonean A repeatable profile format for entrepreneurs, educators, artists, farmers, professionals, community leaders, students, athletes and people whose work deserves wider attention. Nominate someone Great stories are often hiding in plain sight. We are building a nomination pathway so Sierra Leoneans at home and abroad can point us toward people worth knowing. How to contribute People across the platform A person’s story can belong to more than one pillar. We organize coverage by subject, but people are the thread connecting the whole platform. Next Generation James Yambasu case study Skills, portfolio, visibility and opportunity. Business The builders behind five startup ideas Follow the people trying to turn problems into products. Archive The 2020 Conversations An earlier Sierra Leone To The Top people-and-culture series. People & Stories Kelvin Doe kept building. From scrap-built electronics and global attention to a 2026 student robotics tournament in Freetown: a maker-to-mentor story. Read the profile What we look for Do not stop the story at recognition. The better question is what happened next: what was built, who benefited, what became difficult and what others can learn."},{"url":"privacy.html","title":"Privacy","h1":"Privacy","description":"Privacy information for the Sierra Leone To The Top website.","category":"Page","text":"Site policy Privacy Plain-language information about what this website currently does — and does not — collect. Current build Effective September 3, 2026. This policy should be updated whenever the site adds forms, analytics, newsletters, advertising technology or other data-processing services. No advertising or analytics trackers in the current site code The current Sierra Leone To The Top website does not include advertising pixels, third-party analytics scripts, user accounts or onsite tracking cookies. We are deliberately starting simple. Hosting and basic technical data The site is designed to be hosted through GitHub Pages. Like most web-hosting infrastructure, the hosting provider may process technical request information needed to deliver and secure the service. That processing is governed by the provider’s own policies rather than by custom tracking code added by Sierra Leone To The Top. Site search The website’s search feature uses a static index downloaded with the site and performs matching inside your browser. Sierra Leone To The Top does not send search terms to a search provider, analytics service or advertising platform in the current build. RSS and JSON feeds The site may publish public RSS and JSON feeds containing story titles, summaries, categories, dates and public article links. These feeds do not contain subscriber profiles or private reader data. External links The site links to external services such as YouTube, X and source websites. If you follow an external link, that service may collect information under its own privacy policy. Sierra Leone To The Top does not control the data practices of external sites. Story leads and communications The current website does not operate an onsite submission form. If you choose to contact Sierra Leone To The Top through an external social platform, information you send is handled through that platform. When a permanent domain email or submission system is introduced, this policy will be updated to explain how those communications are handled. Children This site is a general public information and media platform. It is not designed to collect personal information directly from children through accounts or forms. Changes to this policy New functionality can change privacy practices. We will revise this page when material data-collection features are introduced rather than leaving an outdated policy in place. Contact Sierra Leone To The Top →"},{"url":"standards.html","title":"Editorial Standards","h1":"Editorial Standards","description":"Editorial standards, verification, corrections, media rights, sponsorship and AI-assisted production policy for Sierra Leone To The Top.","category":"Page","text":"Trust & accountability Editorial Standards Optimism is stronger when people can trust the facts underneath it. Our standard Publish constructively. Verify carefully. Correct openly. Verification Archive Corrections Media rights Sponsorship AI-assisted tools Nonpartisanship Sierra Leone To The Top exists to promote progress without becoming propaganda. That requires a clear line between what we know, what someone claims, what we infer, and what still needs verification. 01 Verification & sourcing Factual claims should be supported by direct evidence, credible public records, named sources, firsthand reporting or authoritative organizations. Where a source is useful to readers, we link to it. We do not manufacture partnerships, achievements, statistics, biographies or quotes. If something cannot yet be verified, we either say so or leave it out. 02 Archive material Older Sierra Leone To The Top material is valuable historical evidence, but it is not automatically current reporting. Archive material is labeled with its date and context. We do not add new biographies or claims to an old title simply because a name appears in the archive. 03 Corrections When a material factual error is confirmed, we correct the public content rather than silently protecting the mistake. Significant corrections should be noted on the affected story where practical. Until the official editorial email is connected, correction leads can be sent through a connected Sierra Leone To The Top social channel. 04 Photographs, video & contributed media We use media we created, control, licensed, received with permission, or are otherwise legally entitled to publish. Third-party media should carry appropriate credit or source information. Contributors must have the right to provide material they submit. Editorial context should not falsely imply that a person, business or organization endorses Sierra Leone To The Top. 05 Sponsorship & commercial relationships A business may support Sierra Leone To The Top, but payment cannot secretly purchase favorable editorial treatment. Sponsored or paid content must be clearly labeled. Editorial profiles should be selected because the story is worthwhile, not because someone paid to appear. 06 AI-assisted tools Technology, including AI-assisted tools, may support research organization, transcription, translation, editing, design, coding or production. The same verification standard applies regardless of which tools help produce the work. AI-generated text, images or summaries should not be used to fabricate documentary evidence, quotations, people, events or partnerships. Material presented as a real photograph or real-world record must be grounded in real source material. 07 Independent & nonpartisan Sierra Leone To The Top is not the communications arm of a political party, government or opposition movement. Public policy and institutions may be covered when relevant to national progress, but the platform does not exist to campaign for candidates or parties. Constructive criticism is compatible with national pride. So is acknowledging genuine progress. The promise National pride without lowering the standard of proof."},{"url":"stories.html","title":"Stories","h1":"Stories of Sierra Leone in motion.","description":"Stories, archive features and original reporting from Sierra Leone To The Top.","category":"Page","text":"Editorial hub Stories of Sierra Leone in motion. Original reporting, profiles, places, ideas and carefully labeled material from the Sierra Leone To The Top archive. Published Start with the archive. We are relaunching with material we can document, then expanding into new reporting across all eight pillars. Communities · Freetown · April 2026 Lumley Beach: progress you can see. A visible shoreline result and a simple editorial principle: progress is worth documenting. Read the story → People + Culture · Archive · 2020 The 2020 Conversations An early Sierra Leone To The Top live-video run preserved on the existing YouTube channel. Explore the archive → Discover · Sourced guide Six ways to discover Sierra Leone A first look across city, conservation, heritage, rainforest, islands and national park. Read the guide → Business Radar · September 2026 Five startups. Five different problems to solve. What READY Salone’s 2026 top-five announcement says about the kinds of problems local founders are tackling. Read the briefing → Next Generation · Case study Training was only the first step. Four practical lessons from one reported Learn2Earn employment outcome. Read the case study → Living Culture · Language Krio: a language of connection. From distinctive Freetown history to an everyday bridge across Sierra Leone. Read the explainer → Diaspora · Heritage Across the Atlantic: Bunce Island and the meaning of return. Gullah Geechee connections, homecoming visits and the role of memory in Sierra Leone’s global story. Read the feature → Ideas & Solutions · Method How We Move Forward: a five-part test for solutions. A framework for moving from complaint to evidence, constraints and measurable action. Read the method → Archive · 2017 The early video-blog era. Preserved Facebook posts document Sierra Leone To The Top publishing public video-blog material in July 2017. Explore 2017 → People & Stories · 2026 Kelvin Doe kept building. From teenage maker to mentor of students experimenting with robotics in Freetown. Read the profile → Editorial desks What we are building next. These are coverage areas, not invented stories. Each desk becomes public content only when the reporting is ready. People Meet a Sierra Leonean Profiles of people building, creating, teaching, leading and solving. Business Built in Sierra Leone Founders, products, makers and enterprises with a real operating story to tell. Culture Living Culture Music, food, languages, art, fashion, history and identity. Diaspora Across the World Return stories, expertise, investment, mentoring and global Sierra Leonean networks. Youth Next Generation Skills, careers, education, technology and verified opportunities. Ideas How We Move Forward Constructive explainers that separate problems, evidence, options and action."},{"url":"story-2017-video-blog.html","title":"The 2017 Video Blog","h1":"Before the relaunch, there was a video blog.","description":"A documented 2017 Sierra Leone To The Top Facebook video-blog archive showing public use of the brand years before the current relaunch.","category":"Archive","text":"From the archive · 2017 Before the relaunch, there was a video blog. Preserved public Facebook links document Sierra Leone To The Top publishing video-blog material in July 2017 — nearly a decade before the platform you see today. Brand archive · Public Facebook evidence preserved August 2026 Archive rule This page records titles, dates and public links preserved in the brand-evidence index. It does not invent descriptions of footage we have not re-reviewed. Explore the archive → A documented public footprint The 2017 material establishes a simple point: Sierra Leone To The Top was already being used publicly as a media identity long before the 2026 rebuild. July 4, 2017 Time For a Haircut Preserved public Facebook video-blog link. Open archive link ↗ July 6, 2017 Master Palm Wine Tapper — 008 Preserved public Facebook video-blog link. Open archive link ↗ July 8, 2017 Crossing That Swamp — 010 Preserved public Facebook video-blog link. Open archive link ↗ July 17, 2017 Freetown Ferry — 019 Preserved public Facebook video-blog link from the same numbered series. Open archive link ↗ What carries forward The format has changed, but the instinct is recognizable: use media to show Sierra Leone through everyday people, places and experiences. The 2026 platform adds a stronger editorial system — verification, corrections, source notes, archive labels and a clear line between original reporting and historical material. Preserve the history. Raise the standard. Continue to the 2020 Conversations →"},{"url":"story-2020-conversations.html","title":"The 2020 Conversations","h1":"The 2020 Conversations.","description":"The 2020 Sierra Leone To The Top live-video archive: an early series of conversations preserved on YouTube.","category":"Archive","text":"From the archive · 2020 The 2020 Conversations. Before the current relaunch, Sierra Leone To The Top was already using live video to create a space for conversation. The existing YouTube archive preserves a run of IG Live titles from May and June 2020. Historical archive · Titles preserved from the existing channel Archive standard This page records what the channel itself preserves. We do not add biographies, claims or context about guests until those details are independently reviewed. Open the YouTube archive → An early version of the idea The format was simple: conversations under the Sierra Leone To The Top name, published during a concentrated stretch in 2020. That history matters because the 2026 platform is not starting from a blank page. It is building on an earlier instinct to connect people and give Sierra Leonean voices a platform. Archive first. Add context only when we can verify it. Preserved episode titles May 19, 2020 AJ Money Archived Sierra Leone To The Top IG Live title preserved on the YouTube channel. May 20, 2020 Sour Fight Archived Sierra Leone To The Top IG Live title preserved on the YouTube channel. May 21, 2020 Lil Skanky Archived Sierra Leone To The Top IG Live title preserved on the YouTube channel. May 22, 2020 TheRealBamSavage Archived Sierra Leone To The Top IG Live title preserved on the YouTube channel. May 25, 2020 M.I.C. Archived Sierra Leone To The Top IG Live title preserved on the YouTube channel. May 26, 2020 Rich Blink Archived Sierra Leone To The Top IG Live title preserved on the YouTube channel. May 27, 2020 Adfega Jr. Archived Sierra Leone To The Top IG Live title preserved on the YouTube channel. May 28, 2020 EDJ Archived Sierra Leone To The Top IG Live title preserved on the YouTube channel. June 3, 2020 KingSLZee Archived Sierra Leone To The Top IG Live title preserved on the YouTube channel. June 4, 2020 ZenoSalone Archived Sierra Leone To The Top IG Live title preserved on the YouTube channel. Beyond the live series The channel also preserves a later video titled Memuna Barnes Jackson “SURVIVED: The Journey” . It remains part of the historical collection and can be developed into a fuller archive entry after the original episode context is reviewed. What changes in the relaunch The archive stays visible, but new Sierra Leone To The Top video will be unmistakably original: our interviews, field footage, narration, reporting, commentary and editing. Historical material will be clearly labeled as archive material rather than presented as new reporting. Explore People & Stories → Explore Culture & Identity →"},{"url":"story-bunce-gullah-homecoming.html","title":"Across the Atlantic: Bunce Island and the meaning of return","h1":"Some connections cross centuries.","description":"Bunce Island, Gullah Geechee heritage and the history of homecoming visits connecting Sierra Leone with descendants across the Atlantic.","category":"Story","text":"Diaspora · Heritage Some connections cross centuries. Bunce Island is not only a place in Sierra Leone’s past. It has also become part of a continuing relationship between Sierra Leone and Gullah Geechee communities in the United States. Heritage explainer · Source-based introduction Core sources Sierra Leone Heritage documents Bunce Island and the homecoming visits. The U.S. National Park Service provides context on Gullah Geechee history and living culture. Sierra Leone Heritage ↗ Gullah Geechee Cultural Heritage Corridor ↗ Bunce Island sits in the Sierra Leone River, upriver from Freetown. Its ruins preserve the history of a British slave-trading fortress through which captive Africans were forced into the transatlantic slave trade. The site is painful because it records departure. Its modern history also includes return. Why the connection matters Enslaved Africans taken from the wider West African rice-growing region were transported to the coastal Lowcountry of what became the United States. The U.S. National Park Service describes the Gullah Geechee as descendants of West and Central Africans enslaved on rice, indigo and Sea Island cotton plantations along the lower Atlantic coast, where communities retained and developed distinctive language, foodways, crafts, spiritual traditions and other cultural practices. Sierra Leone occupies a particular place within that larger history. Sierra Leone’s UNESCO tentative-list submission for Bunce Island describes the site as evidence of cultural interchange between Sierra Leone and the United States and specifically points to Gullah communities in South Carolina and Georgia with Sierra Leonean roots. Sierra Leone Bunce Island departure · memory · heritage Atlantic United States Gullah Geechee survival · culture · connection Homecoming is part of the record Sierra Leone Heritage records a Gullah “Homecoming” visit to Sierra Leone and Bunce Island in 1989, followed by additional homecoming visits in 1997 and 2005. These journeys matter because they turn historical research into lived encounter: descendants returning to a place connected to forced departure. 1948 Bunce Island became Sierra Leone’s first officially protected historic site, according to Sierra Leone Heritage. 1989 A Gullah homecoming group visited Sierra Leone and toured Bunce Island. 1997 Sierra Leone Heritage records another Gullah homecoming visit. 2005 A further homecoming again connected visitors with Bunce Island. Diaspora is also memory When Sierra Leone To The Top talks about the diaspora, it is easy to focus on remittances, investment, professional networks and return migration. Those are important. But diaspora connection can also mean recovering history, protecting cultural memory and creating space for descendants whose relationship with Sierra Leone began through forced displacement rather than voluntary migration. That changes the question from “What can the diaspora give Sierra Leone?” to something more reciprocal: “What histories, identities and relationships can Sierra Leone and its global descendants understand together?” Where Krio and Gullah enter the conversation The relationship is broader than one monument. Scholars have also examined linguistic and cultural connections across the Atlantic. Smithsonian collections preserve work by linguist Lorenzo Dow Turner, who studied both Gullah and Krio and helped demonstrate the depth of African influence in Gullah language and culture. Read: Krio — a language of connection → Editorial note: Gullah Geechee ancestry is connected to many parts of West and Central Africa, not Sierra Leone alone. This story focuses on the documented Sierra Leone connection without claiming a single origin for all Gullah Geechee people or traditions. Explore the Diaspora pillar → Discover Bunce Island →"},{"url":"story-how-we-move-forward.html","title":"How We Move Forward: a five-part test for solutions","h1":"Complaints identify pain. Solutions require more work.","description":"The Sierra Leone To The Top framework for constructive Ideas & Solutions coverage: define the problem, establish evidence, study what exists, confront constraints, and measure action.","category":"Story","text":"How We Move Forward · Method Complaints identify pain. Solutions require more work. Ideas & Solutions will use a five-part test to move public discussion from “something is wrong” toward evidence, realistic options and action that can be measured. Sierra Leone To The Top editorial framework The rule A strong solution story should make the problem clearer without pretending the answer is easier than it is. Read our editorial standards → Constructive does not mean positive all the time. It means the discussion has somewhere to go. A problem may be serious. A policy may be failing. An idea may be promising. The work is to separate those statements and test each one. 01 Define What exactly is the problem? “Education is bad” is too broad to solve. Which students? Which level? Attendance, teaching quality, materials, exams, employability, infrastructure or something else? A useful problem statement is narrow enough to investigate. 02 Evidence What do we actually know? Separate measurements, records, firsthand reporting and credible research from assumptions, anecdotes and political talking points. If evidence is incomplete, say that rather than filling the gap with certainty. 03 Learn What is already being tried? Before proposing a new project, look for existing Sierra Leonean efforts, previous programs, local expertise and relevant examples elsewhere. Reinventing something that already exists can waste money and attention. 04 Reality What could stop the idea from working? Cost, maintenance, electricity, staffing, procurement, regulation, geography, public behavior, politics and institutional capacity can all turn an attractive idea into a poor implementation. Constraints belong inside the solution, not in the fine print. 05 Act + measure What is the smallest useful next step? Define who must act, what can be tested, what success would look like and what evidence would tell us to stop, change or expand. The goal is not a perfect national master plan every time. It may be a credible pilot, a better question or a decision supported by evidence. The five questions Problem What exactly are we trying to change? Evidence How do we know the problem exists in the form described? Existing work Who is already trying something? Constraints What makes implementation difficult? Next action What can be tested and measured? What this prevents This method is designed to resist two common failures: cynicism that stops at criticism, and optimism that jumps directly to a shiny solution. Both can avoid the hard middle — evidence, tradeoffs and implementation. Problem → Evidence → Existing work → Constraints → Action + Measurement Where we will use it Energy. Transportation. Waste. Education. Tourism. Agriculture. Housing. Technology. Health systems. Public services. Governance. Any subject can enter the Ideas & Solutions desk if we can make the question specific enough and treat competing evidence fairly. Editorial note: this is a reporting framework, not a claim that every public problem can be reduced to five simple steps. Complex issues may require competing perspectives, longer evidence reviews and multiple possible actions. Explore Ideas & Solutions →"},{"url":"story-kelvin-doe-maker-mentor.html","title":"Kelvin Doe: from maker to mentor","h1":"Kelvin Doe kept building.","description":"Kelvin Doe's journey from self-taught Freetown maker to mentor of the next generation of Sierra Leonean students.","category":"People","text":"People & Stories · Maker to mentor Kelvin Doe kept building. The world first noticed a teenage inventor making electronics from discarded parts in Freetown. More than a decade later, the more interesting question is what happens when that story becomes a platform for the next generation. Source-based profile · September 2026 Sources This profile uses Sierra Leone's Ministry of Communication, Technology and Innovation for the 2026 robotics program and contemporary 2012–2013 reporting for Doe's early maker story. MoCTI · Jan. 10, 2026 ↗ ABC News · Dec. 4, 2012 ↗ Fast Company · May 13, 2013 ↗ Kelvin Doe became internationally known as a teenager after teaching himself electronics in Freetown and building batteries, generators, radio equipment and a community radio station from salvaged materials. Contemporary reporting connected that work to local innovation competitions and a visit to MIT. The part of the story that matters now Recognition can become a dead end if a young innovator remains frozen forever as “the talented teenager.” Doe's 2026 work points in a different direction. In January 2026, Sierra Leone's technology ministry reported that the Kelvin Doe Foundation hosted a student robotics tournament at Prince of Wales School in Freetown, Doe's alma mater. Students programmed robot cars, worked in teams and competed through hands-on problem solving. 2012 Maker International attention followed Doe's scrap-built electronics and radio work. 2026 Mentor His foundation returned to a Sierra Leonean school environment to put robotics in students' hands. A better measure of success The strongest lesson is not “become famous at 15.” That is not a useful development model. A more useful model is: curiosity → access → practice → visibility → opportunity → return value to others. Curiosity Start by trying to understand how things work. Practice Build before everything is perfect. Access Competitions, mentors and institutions can widen the field of possibility. Return Success becomes more valuable when it creates opportunity for others. Why this belongs on Sierra Leone To The Top Kelvin Doe's story fits the platform because it connects several pillars at once: People, Youth, Innovation and Diaspora experience. It also shows why progress stories should not stop at the moment a Sierra Leonean receives international attention. The better follow-up is: what did they build next, who benefited, what became difficult, and what can another person learn from the path? Editorial note: we have not independently audited every historical claim about Doe's early inventions or the 2026 foundation program. Where facts come from the ministry or other profiles, they are treated as attributed source material rather than as our firsthand reporting. Explore People & Stories → Explore Youth & Next Generation →"},{"url":"story-krio-language-connection.html","title":"Krio: a language of connection","h1":"Krio is more than a way of speaking.","description":"An introduction to Krio as a language of identity and connection in Sierra Leone, grounded in Sierra Leonean heritage and scholarly sources.","category":"Story","text":"Living Culture · Language Krio is more than a way of speaking. It carries a distinctive Freetown history while also serving as one of Sierra Leone’s strongest everyday bridges across communities. Culture explainer · Source-based introduction Sources This introduction draws on Sierra Leone’s Ministry of Tourism and Cultural Affairs, Sierra Leone Heritage, the Permanent Mission of Sierra Leone and academic collections. Tourism Ministry ↗ Sierra Leone Heritage ↗ Sierra Leone is linguistically diverse. English is the official language used in government and formal education, while Mende, Temne and many other Sierra Leonean languages remain central to community identity. Krio occupies a different kind of space: it is widely used as a common language across groups. A language shaped by Freetown Sierra Leone Heritage traces Krio society to the Black settlers and liberated Africans who came together in Freetown and the peninsula during the nineteenth century, creating a distinctive culture shaped by influences from West Africa, Europe and the Americas. The language developed within that history. Sierra Leone Heritage describes it as a creole that grew out of earlier West African coast-English contact and absorbed vocabulary from African languages, including Yoruba. The bigger idea A local language became a national bridge. The Ministry of Tourism identifies Krio as a language that helps unite Sierra Leone’s many ethnic communities in trade and everyday interaction. Why “just broken English” misses the point Krio has sometimes been dismissed as informal or defective English. Linguistic scholarship treats it as a creole language with its own history, structure and social role. Academic work has examined Krio not only as a language but as part of debates about national identity, education and integration in Sierra Leone. A language with Atlantic connections Krio’s history also makes it part of a wider Atlantic story. Smithsonian collections include fieldwork by African American linguist Lorenzo Dow Turner, who studied Krio in Sierra Leone in 1951 because of its historical and linguistic connections with Gullah, a creole language spoken in coastal communities of the southeastern United States. A few points of entry Sierra Leone’s Permanent Mission gives visitors simple examples such as “kusheh” for hello and “tenki” for thank you. But Krio is not a tourist phrasebook. The real invitation is to hear how people use the language — its humor, rhythm, proverbs, emphasis and ability to shift between intimacy, business, politics, music and everyday life. Identity Rooted in a distinctive Krio historical community. Connection Used across Sierra Leone’s linguistic diversity. History Shaped by West African and Atlantic encounters. Living culture Continuously changing through daily use. What we want to explore next A good culture platform should not explain Krio only through outsiders or old books. Future Sierra Leone To The Top work should include native speakers, linguists, writers, musicians, teachers and young people — and should also give space to Sierra Leone’s many other languages rather than treating one language as the whole country. Editorial note: language history is complex and scholarly interpretations of creole origins are not identical. This article is an introduction, not a definitive linguistic history. We use “Krio” for both the language and, where context requires, the historical community, while recognizing that those are related but not interchangeable meanings. Read: Bunce Island, Gullah Geechee heritage and the meaning of return → Explore Culture & Identity →"},{"url":"story-learn2earn-lessons.html","title":"From training to an opportunity","h1":"Training was only the first step.","description":"A source-based look at what one Sierra Leone Learn2Earn case study suggests about turning digital-skills training into an employment outcome.","category":"Story","text":"Next Generation · Case study Training was only the first step. A July 2026 Learn2Earn story published by Sierra Leone’s technology ministry points to something useful: the employment outcome came from combining skills with a portfolio, professional visibility and follow-through. Source-based analysis · September 2026 The source MoCTI profiled James Emmanuel Paul Yambasu, then a second-year Mathematics and Statistics student at Fourah Bay College, after his Learn2Earn Cohort 2 training. Read the MoCTI profile ↗ According to the Ministry’s profile, the training program helped participants build practical digital-work assets rather than collect a certificate alone. Yambasu worked on professional profiles and a portfolio, then publicly shared his work. The Ministry says that visibility contributed to a referral shortly after the cohort ended and led to a personal-assistant role supporting climate-informed disease-surveillance work. Sierra Leone To The Top has not independently verified the employment relationship described by the Ministry. But the sequence in the public case study is still useful because it highlights behaviors that young people can apply even outside one program. 01 Skills need evidence. Knowing how to do something and showing that you can do it are different. A portfolio turns abstract ability into something another person can evaluate. 02 Visibility is part of employability. The case study emphasizes LinkedIn, Upwork and a portfolio website. The larger lesson is not that one platform guarantees work; it is that useful skills create more opportunity when the right people can discover them. 03 Existing knowledge can be packaged better. Yambasu’s university background already included quantitative and research skills. The training appears to have helped convert those capabilities into a clearer professional presentation. 04 Mentors can shorten the distance to opportunity. The Ministry’s profile says a mentor alerted him to the program. That is a small detail with a large implication: networks often matter before the application even begins. What a useful opportunity desk should do This is why the Sierra Leone To The Top Youth desk should not merely repost scholarship or training announcements. When possible, we should also explain what successful applicants actually did, what skills employers noticed, what evidence applicants needed and what happened after the program ended. What do you learn? Specific skills, not vague promises. What do you build? Portfolio, project, credential or work sample. Who sees it? Employers, clients, mentors or collaborators. What comes next? A pathway beyond the training itself. Editorial note: facts about the participant and program above are attributed to the Ministry’s published profile. Our four lessons are Sierra Leone To The Top’s analysis of that reported sequence, not claims made by the participant. Explore Youth & Next Generation →"},{"url":"story-lumley-beach.html","title":"From the Archive: Lumley Beach","h1":"Lumley Beach: progress you can see.","description":"From the Archive: a Lumley Beach image from April 2026 and what visible local progress can teach us.","category":"Story","text":"From the archive · Freetown Lumley Beach: progress you can see. A field image dated April 23, 2026 and labeled as a clean-shoreline result at Lumley Beach captures a simple idea: visible improvements matter, and they are worth documenting. Freetown · Sierra Leone · April 2026 Lumley Beach, Freetown · Photo: Sierra Leone To The Top archive. Why it belongs here Sierra Leone To The Top looks for practical signs of progress — not only big announcements. Sometimes a story is a person. Sometimes it is a business, an idea or a place. And sometimes it is a visible result: a public space that looks better than it did before. This photograph is useful because it captures an outcome rather than a promise. The larger story behind any community improvement deserves verification, attribution and context, but the result itself is a reminder that progress can be local, practical and immediately visible. What we want to document Across Sierra Leone, people are solving problems every day. This platform will look for the people behind those efforts, what they tried, what changed, what can be learned and whether the idea can travel. See it Notice useful change. Verify it Separate evidence from claims. Share it Give good work wider visibility. Learn from it Ask what others can use. A growing national archive “From the Archive” will preserve photographs, stories and moments that help show Sierra Leone in motion — while clearly distinguishing what is verified, what is historical context and what still needs further reporting. Explore Communities & Development →"},{"url":"story-ready-salone-startups.html","title":"Five startups, five problems to solve","h1":"Five startups. Five different problems to solve.","description":"Five startups recognized at READY Salone Investor Day 2026 show the range of problems Sierra Leonean entrepreneurs are trying to solve.","category":"Story","text":"Business Radar · September 2026 Five startups. Five different problems to solve. A recent READY Salone Investor Day offers a useful snapshot of the range of businesses emerging from Sierra Leone — from tools for market traders to localized artificial intelligence. Source-based briefing · Not an endorsement or ranking by Sierra Leone To The Top What happened Sierra Leone’s Ministry of Communication, Technology and Innovation said 11 investment-ready startups pitched at READY Salone Investor Day. Five were selected for international technology events. MoCTI news source ↗ The most interesting part of the announcement is not simply who placed first. It is the variety of problems the finalists are trying to solve. The five businesses named by the Ministry operate across financial tools for small businesses, clean energy, urban waste, agriculture and local-language technology. 01 Small-business tools MiBuks The Ministry describes MiBuks as a Freetown-based digital platform for market traders and micro, small and medium-sized businesses. Its tools include trading records, expense tracking, inventory management and credit scoring intended to help businesses build financial records and access inventory financing. MoCTI announced MiBuks as first place and selected it for COMEUP Festival 2026 in South Korea. 02 Bioenergy Greentech Bioenergy Greentech Bioenergy is described as developing biogas digester technology designed to turn biodegradable waste into cooking gas and organic fertilizer. MoCTI announced Greentech Bioenergy as second place and selected it alongside MiBuks for COMEUP Festival 2026. 03 Urban climate technology GreenBin The Ministry says GreenBin is working on smart waste collection, community waste reporting and organic fertilizer production, combining urban-service problems with environmental technology. GreenBin was among three startups the Ministry said were selected for Africa Tech Festival in South Africa. 04 Climate-smart agriculture GCAD Green Compost in Agriculture for Development (GCAD) is described as turning organic market waste and coastal seaweed into compost fertilizer, linking waste recovery with soil health, agriculture and employment. GCAD was also announced for Africa Tech Festival. 05 Localized artificial intelligence Geneline-X Geneline-X is building localized language technology. The Ministry highlights Krio-to-English speech recognition and no-code chatbot tools designed for low-connectivity environments. Geneline-X completed the five startups named for international technology events. Why this matters The five businesses are not proof that Sierra Leone’s startup ecosystem has solved its financing, infrastructure or scaling challenges. They are evidence of something narrower and still important: entrepreneurs are attempting to build products around problems that are deeply local — market-trader recordkeeping, organic waste, urban services, agricultural inputs and language access. That is the kind of innovation Sierra Leone To The Top wants to follow: not technology for the sake of appearing modern, but tools whose value can be explained in terms of a real user and a real problem. Source note: company descriptions and competition results above are summarized from the Sierra Leone Ministry of Communication, Technology and Innovation’s August 3, 2026 announcement and related public material. Sierra Leone To The Top has not independently audited company performance, financing, product claims or future event attendance. What we want to ask next Who uses it? Talk to customers, not only founders. Does it work? Look for evidence beyond pitch language. Can it scale? Understand operating constraints in Sierra Leone. What can others learn? Turn a company profile into a useful lesson. Explore Business & Innovation →"},{"url":"watch.html","title":"Watch","h1":"Watch Sierra Leone To The Top.","description":"Watch Sierra Leone To The Top video stories, interviews, travel, culture and ideas.","category":"Page","text":"Video hub Watch Sierra Leone To The Top. Interviews, travel, culture, business, community stories and ideas — built around Sierra Leonean voices. Sierra Leone To The Top TV A channel for seeing Sierra Leone through the people who know it, build it and live it. Long-form interviews, field stories, travel, culture, business profiles and constructive explainers. Visit the YouTube channel Series Meet a Sierra Leonean Conversations with people building, creating and contributing. Series Discover Sierra Leone Places, food, travel, culture and local experiences. Series How We Move Forward Constructive explainers and solution-focused conversations. Our video standard Original by design. Sierra Leone To The Top video will be built around original interviews, field footage, narration, reporting, commentary and editing. When archival or contributed material is used, its role and source should be clear. The goal is not simply to show Sierra Leone. It is to help people understand it. Every strong video should be able to become more: a website feature, short clips, photographs, quotes and useful social content — all pointing back to the larger story. Archive The media history reaches beyond YouTube. Preserved Facebook evidence documents a Sierra Leone To The Top Video Blog in 2017, while the existing YouTube channel preserves a concentrated IG Live series from 2020. Explore the full archive →"},{"url":"youth.html","title":"Youth & the Next Generation","h1":"Youth & Next Generation","description":"Education, skills, careers, technology and opportunity for young Sierra Leoneans.","category":"Page","text":"Core pillar Youth & Next Generation What opportunities can we open for what comes next? Opportunity desk This section is designed to turn attention into opportunity: education, digital skills, technical training, careers, entrepreneurship, mentorship and useful exposure to the wider world. An opportunity page is only useful if the information is current. Our verification rule Time-sensitive opportunities will show the source, eligibility, deadline and the date we last checked them. Expired items should move out of the active list rather than becoming stale clutter. Source Where did it come from? Eligibility Who can apply? Deadline When does it close? Checked When did we verify it? Next generation Build capability, not just inspiration. Motivation has a place. Practical pathways matter more. Learn Education Scholarships, courses and pathways explained clearly. Skill Digital & technical AI, software, trades and practical skills tied to real work. Work Careers What jobs require, how professions work and how to prepare. Build Entrepreneurship Starting small, testing ideas and learning how businesses operate. Connect Mentorship Useful relationships between experienced people and emerging talent. See Global exposure Show young Sierra Leoneans possibilities they may not yet have encountered. Next Generation · Case study Training was only the first step. A recent Learn2Earn profile offers four useful lessons about portfolios, professional visibility, existing skills and mentorship. Read the case study Opportunity desk principle Ask what happens after the certificate. Programs are most useful when young people can see the pathway from learning to evidence, visibility, work and continued growth. People + Next Generation From young maker to mentor. Kelvin Doe's story gives this desk a useful long view: opportunity matters, but so does what a successful young innovator later builds for others. Read the Kelvin Doe profile"}];
